<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Image_1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the image set \({\rm im}(f)\) of the function \[ {@f1@}. \]</p>
<p>Give your answer as an inequality in \(y\), for example <code>y&lt;=5</code>. Type <code>&lt;=</code> for \(\le\) and <code>&gt;=</code> for \(\ge\).</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>The <strong>image set</strong> (range) of \(f\) is the set of all output values. For a quadratic, <strong>completing the square</strong> shows it directly. Halve the coefficient of \(x\) and write</p>
\[ {@rhs@} = {@(x+n1)^2+n2@}. \]
<p>A square is never negative, so \({@(x+n1)^2@} \ge 0\) for every \(x\), and therefore \(f(x) \ge {@n2@}\). The value \({@n2@}\) is actually reached, at \(x={@-n1@}\), where the square is \(0\). So the smallest output is \({@n2@}\), and every larger value is also an output.</p>
<p>Hence \({\rm im}(f) = \{\,y : y \ge {@n2@}\,\}\), and the answer is \({@correctanswer@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
